WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.380
Access control is part of API design.

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:11.480
An interface that can reach sensitive state, privileged operations, or tenant-scoped data should make authority explicit enough to review and test.

3
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.080
Avoid ambient authority where possible.

4
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:25.700
If an API silently reads broad global state, process-wide credentials, or hidden context, callers may gain more authority than the operation actually needs.

5
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.620
Capability-style handles can narrow authority.

6
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:36.400
Instead of passing a general service object to every caller, provide a scoped object that allows only the operation and context required.

7
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.700
This makes misuse harder and review easier.

8
00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:45.100
Authorization checks should be close enough to the boundary that they are difficult to skip accidentally.

9
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.400
A lower-level helper may still need checks if it can be called from multiple entry points with different trust assumptions.

10
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.160
Tenant, user, environment, and role boundaries should be represented deliberately.

11
00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:08.460
A function that accepts a tenant-scoped handle or validated authorization context is easier to reason about than one that accepts a raw identifier and looks up authority elsewhere.

12
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.620
Powerful operations should be hard to call casually.

13
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:21.800
Destructive actions, sensitive reads, configuration changes, and credential-related operations should require explicit authority and clear failure behavior.

14
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:32.240
The review question is whether the API receives only the authority it needs, applies it consistently, and prevents callers from accidentally reaching broader power through convenience paths.

15
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.160
Context objects should be scoped carefully.

16
00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:42.460
A context that carries every permission in the process is not least privilege; it is another form of ambient authority.

17
00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:51.620
Capability objects are most useful when they are hard to forge, narrow in purpose, and tied to the operation or resource they authorize.

18
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:58.460
Tests should cover denied access and cross-context calls, not only successful calls from trusted code.

19
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.660
Least privilege is strongest when the API makes the smaller authority easier to pass than the broader one.
